WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.220
ASP.NET Core Identity uses token providers for account workflows such as email confirmation, password reset, change email or phone, and two-factor flows.

2
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:14.360
These tokens support account lifecycle actions.

3
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:20.780
They should be purpose-specific, time-limited, and handled according to the sensitivity of the workflow.

4
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.020
Password reset and email confirmation tokens are not API access tokens.

5
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:31.800
They have a different purpose, issuer, validation path, and operational risk.

6
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:38.940
A reset token may allow an account workflow to proceed, while an API access token is intended for resource access decisions.

7
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.760
Mixing these concepts makes review harder and can produce unsafe assumptions.

8
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:51.880
Token links and token values should not be logged, copied into analytics, displayed unnecessarily, or exposed through support artifacts.

9
00:00:51.980 --> 00:01:04.460
Email links, support tickets, screenshots, page telemetry, and failed request logs can all become accidental exposure paths if the application records full values without redaction.

10
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:10.420
ASP.NET Core Identity token behavior can depend on Data Protection configuration.

11
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:16.040
Data Protection protects and validates framework or application payloads using a key ring.

12
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:24.620
If the key ring is lost, inconsistent, or environment-confused, protected token behavior may change in ways that affect account workflows.

13
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:33.220
Multi-instance deployments need Data Protection key persistence and consistent application identity so protected tokens can be validated reliably.

14
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:41.240
Containers, multiple servers, deployment slots, and cloud hosting all affect where keys live and which application can read them.

15
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.760
Token failure handling should be predictable and safe for users and support teams.

16
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:57.700
A user should receive a clear next step when an account workflow token is invalid or expired, while support teams should have safe diagnostic categories rather than raw token values.
